{
 "_source": "opt/irobot/cleantrack/bin/cleantrack, firmware ruby-0.7.12+Firmware-Build+4625 (j9). Embedded JSON Schema blocks, extracted verbatim.",
 "_channel": "LOCAL MQTT ONLY. These schemas describe what the robot accepts and emits on its own channel. They are NOT the contract for the same events as the cloud serialises them, and the two differ in both container and field names. The local timeline container is `eventArray`; the cloud mission history uses `finEvents`, which appears nowhere in this file. ha_roomba_plus's MissionArchive reads the CLOUD form.",
 "_caveat": "cleantrack is built across product lines. A schema proves what the PARSER ACCEPTS, not that a j9 reports the field -- the command enum contains Astro verbs (patrol, sentry, waypoint_*) that this hardware has no use for. Presence here is not evidence of presence on the wire.",
 "_scope": "CLASSIC-generation local vocabulary. Most of it belongs to roombapy, not to this library. Kept here because it is the first vendor contract with types, required lists and value ranges the project has -- something to CHECK against, rather than something to implement.",
 "_a_mistake_made_with_this_file": "On the day it was extracted, `timeline.eventError.error` (required: ['value']) was compared against ha_roomba_plus reading `error.code` out of finEvents, and reported as a wire-key bug -- with the supporting observation that `code` appears in none of these properties. Both facts were right and the conclusion was wrong: one side is the local channel, the other the cloud, and `code` being absent here says nothing about what the cloud sends. The tell was in the code being checked, one comment above the loop: it cites MISSIONSTORE_FIELD_REGISTRY.md, which states the source is the cloud mission history. BEFORE USING THIS FILE AGAINST ANY CODE, establish which channel that code reads. Agreement across the two is a finding; disagreement is the default and means nothing on its own.",
 "_enum_keys": "PATH-QUALIFIED. A first version keyed these on the property name alone, which collapsed every `type` in the file into whichever one was reached first -- JSON Schema uses `type` for its own keyword and for fields of that name. The comparison guard caught it on its first run.",
